ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087898 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bb9db01e-936b-52c3-b219-e1950b5ae188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088189 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088189 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $y = 5x - 4x^{2}$, calculate $y^{′}(x)$ using the definition of the derivative." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46eecd6b-234b-5d91-be76-8bf8679a9744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088823 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088823 #edb/ref 1061 605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088823 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "454777cc-cdc5-5db8-b536-5b674f1199d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088823 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088824 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088824 #edb/ref 1061 606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088824 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37cc5660-640c-5836-92be-6ff27138fdcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088824 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088827 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088827 #edb/ref 1061 609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088827 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b806b59-9344-54a4-8988-8cb76d4286c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088827 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088828 #edb/ref 1061 613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088828 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c77b3ab-7255-54eb-92d4-2ad06b30a0cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088828 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088899 #edb/ref 1064 "The instantaneous rate of change of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a,$ denoted $f'(a),$ is given by the limit\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}.\n$$\n\nThis formula works for a specific point $x=a.$ If we want a method that works for a *general* point $x,$ then we substitute $a=x$ in the formula and get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f'(x)$ is called the **derivative of $f(x)$ with respect to $x$**. It gives the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at a general point with coordinates $(x,f(x)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088899 #edb/ref 1065 327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088899 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "098fd6b0-306b-544a-9e7f-b301ff48f525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088899 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088900 #edb/ref 1064 "For a given curve $y=f(x),$ the derivative can be represented using several different notations. They are\n\n$$\nf'(x),\\qquad y'(x), \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIn words, the term $y'(x)$  is said like\n\n- \"$y$ prime of $x$,\" or\n\n- \"$y$ prime,\" or sometimes\n\n- \"$y$ dash.\"\n\nThe term $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ is said like \"dee $y$ by dee $x$.\"\n\nThe important thing to remember is that they all represent the same thing, the derivative." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088900 #edb/ref 1065 329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088900 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cda9f5ac-1ad1-50ce-8bc4-ee5f3be5fd92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088900 #edb/ref 1067 "Alternative Definitions For the Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
